Subject: [sshmap fork 0007] an erase mode the handler does not know is a no-op (ESC[3K / ESC[4J])

Provenance: the published defect report BUG-C of the pyte audit
            (github.com/org-ai-assisted/pyte-audit, `BUGS/BUG-C-erase-unhandled-how-unboundlocal.md`;
            the tree F:\PythonAI\pyte_org-ai-assisted_fork) — and the FIX is that fork's own: `C` is
            one of the two findings its `pyte-audit-fixes.txt` declares fixed, while upstream master
            (0.8.3.dev) still carries the defect. Re-measured against the vendored fork first:
            `erase_in_line()` builds its local `interval` only for the modes it implements (0/1/2)
            and `erase_in_display()` for 0/1/2/3, then uses the local unconditionally — `ESC[3K`
            and `ESC[4J` raise `UnboundLocalError: cannot access local variable 'interval' where it
            is not associated with a value` out of `feed()`, and the same two sequences raise
            through `TerminalScreen.feed` (`SshmapHistoryScreen`, whose `erase_in_display` override
            calls `super()` first), i.e. the whole chunk after them is lost.
            xterm's ctlseqs defines EL for Ps = 0/1/2 only — the "Erase Saved Lines" Ps = 3 belongs
            to ED (`CSI 3 J`), which the fork already implements through `HistoryScreen` — so
            `ESC[3K` is malformed input and a no-op IS the honest answer. Mapping `EL 3` onto "erase
            the saved lines" was considered and deliberately NOT taken: that is a semantics
            addition, and v1.5.7.1 is a hardening release.
            The `dirty` ordering: the fix validates BEFORE `self.dirty.add(...)` in `erase_in_line`,
            so a refused mode marks no line for redraw — the order `erase_in_display` already uses.
Base:       pyte 0.8.2 (PyPI sdist, sha256 in MANIFEST.md), pyte/screens.py (after patches 0001-0006).
Policy:     drop when upstream merges the report's fix (0.8.3+); the report declares the defect open
            in master. `CSI 0K`/`1K`/`2K`/`3J` keep their behaviour byte for byte, the `HistoryScreen`
            scrollback reset on `ED 3` included.
Applied:    2026-09-26, sshmap v1.5.7.1.

